WB.L("salt-white-gold", "food", "beginner", "Salt: the rock that built civilisations",
  "Empires taxed it, armies guarded it and whole cities grew rich on it. The humble white crystal on your table has one of the biggest stories in food.",
  [
    ["The mineral we crave", "Our bodies need sodium for nerves, muscles and water balance, which is why people and animals have always sought out salt.",
      "Salt is mostly sodium chloride, and sodium is essential. Your nerves use it to fire signals, your muscles need it to contract, and your kidneys use it to manage the water in your body. Hunter-gatherers who ate plenty of meat got enough from their food, but once people farmed grain, they needed extra. Animals feel the same pull, and grazing animals wear tracks to natural salt licks. Many ancient roads and settlements grew up along such routes.\n\nToday the problem has flipped. Most Australians eat more salt than health authorities recommend, and much of it is hidden in bread, sauces and processed meats."],
    ["Mines, springs and wells", "Salt has been mined, boiled from springs, dried from sea water and pumped from deep wells.",
      "There are three classic sources. Rock salt is mined from ancient seas that dried out and were buried. At Hallstatt in the Austrian Alps, people have mined it since the Bronze Age, and the region grew rich enough that an early European culture is named after it. Brine from salty springs is boiled down, as across Europe and China. Sea water is dried by sun and wind in shallow ponds. Australia is a major producer this way, with huge salt fields near Dampier in Western Australia, mostly shipped to Asia for industry.\n\nIn Sichuan, workers drilled narrow wells with heavy bits on bamboo cables. In 1835 the Shenhai well at Zigong passed 1,000 metres, and natural gas from such wells fuelled the fires that boiled the brine."],
    ["Salt, tax and power", "Because everyone needs salt, governments taxed and controlled it, and those taxes became symbols of injustice.",
      "Salt was big business for the state. Under the Han dynasty in the 110s BC, China's government took control of salt and iron production, and a court debate in 81 BC, recorded as the Discourses on Salt and Iron, argued whether the state should profit from necessities.\n\nA salt tax is hard to dodge. France's gabelle was uneven, some regions paying many times more than others and some people required to buy a minimum amount. Smugglers were punished harshly, and the gabelle was one grievance of the French Revolution, abolished in 1790. In 1930, Gandhi walked to the sea at Dandi and picked up natural salt in defiance of the British Indian salt monopoly.\n\nThe word salary comes from the Latin salarium, related to sal, meaning salt. But there is no clear evidence that Roman soldiers were paid in salt."],
    ["Salt as a preserver", "Salt preserves food by drawing water out of it, leaving too little free water for most microbes to grow.",
      "Before refrigeration, salt was a main way to stop food rotting. It works through osmosis. Salt pulls water out of the food's cells and out of any bacteria, and the water left is so tightly bound to dissolved salt that most microbes cannot use it. That is how salted cod, cured ham, anchovies and corned beef were made. Salt cod became a huge international trade and is still loved in Portugal, Spain and the Caribbean.\n\nSalt also steers fermentation. In sauerkraut, kimchi and pickles, the right amount holds back spoilage organisms while helpful lactic acid bacteria do their sour, flavourful work."],
    ["Flakes, rocks and iodine", "Nearly all cooking salts are chemically similar, and what matters most is crystal size and added iodine.",
      "Sea salt, rock salt, pink Himalayan salt and table salt are overwhelmingly sodium chloride. Trace minerals tint some salts, but in the amounts you eat they make little nutritional difference. What changes is the crystal. Big flakes dissolve slowly and crunch on top of food, while fine salt packs densely, so a teaspoon of fine salt is noticeably saltier than a teaspoon of flakes. That is why a recipe calling for a teaspoon of salt can come out differently.\n\nIodine is the other difference. Deficiency can cause goitre and affect brain development, so many countries add iodine to salt. Since 2009, Australian bakers have been required to use iodised salt in most bread, a quiet public health measure."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did early farmers need extra salt that hunter-gatherers often did not?", ["Grain supplies little sodium, unlike a meat-rich diet","Farming made their bodies lose sodium faster","Wild animals had eaten all the natural salt","Grain contains compounds that block sodium"], 0, "Meat supplies sodium, so people who settled to farm grain had to find salt elsewhere."],
    [1, "How is salt produced by solar evaporation?", ["Sea water is dried by sun and wind in shallow ponds","Brine from springs is boiled over gas fires","Rock salt is crushed and washed in the sun","Deep wells pump out dry crystals"], 0, "Solar evaporation lets sun and wind do the work, as on Western Australia's coast."],
    [2, "Why did salt taxes become symbols of injustice?", ["Salt was a luxury, so only wealthy households could be taxed","Everyone needed salt, so the tax was hard to avoid and unfair","Governments banned salt, so ordinary people had to smuggle it","The tax was paid only by foreign merchants and their agents"], 1, "A necessity cannot be avoided, so heavy or uneven salt taxes provoked lasting anger."],
    [3, "How does salt help preserve food?", ["It kills microbes by heating the food","It makes food too acidic for bacteria","It draws water out so microbes cannot grow","It coats the food in a layer of oil"], 2, "Through osmosis, salt removes the free water microbes need to grow."],
    [4, "What really differs between sea salt, rock salt and table salt?", ["Their basic chemical makeup, which differs a lot","Mainly crystal size and whether iodine is added","How much sodium each contains in every gram","The trace minerals, which make a big nutritional difference"], 1, "All are mostly sodium chloride, so crystal size and added iodine matter most."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Empires taxed it and a famous march defied it. Why was salt worth that much?"});

WB.L("banana-clone-fruit", "food", "beginner", "Bananas: the fruit of clones",
  "The banana in your lunchbox is almost certainly a genetic copy of nearly every other banana in the shop. That makes it cheap, convenient and surprisingly fragile.",
  [
    ["Not a tree, a berry", "The banana plant is a giant herb with a trunk made of leaves, and its fruit is botanically a berry.",
      "A banana plantation looks like a forest, but banana plants are not trees. They have no wood. What looks like a trunk is a pseudostem of tightly wrapped leaf bases, and the true stem stays mostly underground as a fleshy corm. Some plants grow taller than a house, making them among the largest herbaceous plants in the world. Each pseudostem flowers once, bears one large bunch and dies back, while new shoots called suckers rise from the base.\n\nBotanically, the fruit is a berry, because it develops from a single flower's ovary and has its seeds inside the flesh. Strawberries, confusingly, do not count as true berries."],
    ["Seedless clones", "Cultivated bananas are sterile and seedless, so every new plant is grown from a piece of its parent: a clone.",
      "Wild bananas, which grow across South-East Asia and the western Pacific, are full of hard seeds. Evidence from Kuk Swamp in Papua New Guinea suggests people cultivated bananas there around 7,000 years ago. Traders and migrants carried them to India, Africa and the Pacific, and they reached the Americas after European contact in the early sixteenth century. Sweet bananas are eaten raw, while plantains and cooking bananas are boiled or fried, and in parts of East Africa they are a daily staple.\n\nOur bananas come from rare plants with three sets of chromosomes, which makes them sterile. The dark specks inside are undeveloped seeds. Farmers plant suckers, cuttings or laboratory plantlets, so each plant is identical to its parent."],
    ["Big Mike to Cavendish", "Because export bananas are clones, a soil fungus destroyed the Gros Michel and the Cavendish took its place.",
      "Until the mid-twentieth century, the banana in Western shops was mostly the Gros Michel, or Big Mike. It was large and creamy, and its thick skin survived long shipping. But it was vulnerable to Panama disease, a wilt caused by a soil fungus that blocks the plant's water vessels and can persist in soil for decades. Every Gros Michel was a clone, so none had resistance. Plantations across Central America and the Caribbean were devastated. By the 1960s, exporters had switched to the Cavendish, which resisted that strain.\n\nThe Cavendish has an English story. In the 1830s, the Duke of Devonshire's gardener Joseph Paxton, who later designed the Crystal Palace, coaxed one into fruit in the Chatsworth House hothouses and named it after his employer. It was more delicate, so exporters began packing bananas in boxes."],
    ["How bananas ripen", "Bananas are picked green and ripen as the gas ethylene turns starch into sugar.",
      "Shop bananas are harvested hard and green, because ripe fruit bruises easily. Like apples, tomatoes and avocados, bananas are climacteric fruit: they keep ripening after picking. The trigger is ethylene, a simple gas the fruit produces itself. Distributors use ripening rooms and add a little ethylene to start the fruit off evenly.\n\nAs a banana ripens, starch converts to sugars, the flesh softens and the green chlorophyll in the skin breaks down, revealing yellow pigments. Brown spots mean it is very ripe. A paper bag with an apple traps ethylene and speeds ripening, while refrigeration blackens the skin but leaves the flesh fine."],
    ["Power and Australian bananas", "Banana money shaped Central American politics, and Australia protects its own industry with strict biosecurity.",
      "In the early twentieth century, American companies such as United Fruit owned plantations, railways and ports in Central America. In 1904 O. Henry used the phrase \"banana republic\" for a country dominated by foreign business. In 1928 Colombian troops fired on striking banana workers, and in 1954 a CIA-backed coup removed Guatemala's elected government, whose land reforms threatened United Fruit.\n\nMost Australian bananas grow in far north Queensland, around Tully and Innisfail. Cavendish dominates, and the small, sweet Lady Finger is a local favourite. Fresh bananas are not imported, so cyclones such as Larry in 2006 and Yasi in 2011 sent prices soaring. The biggest threat is Tropical Race 4, a Panama disease strain that attacks Cavendish and was found in Queensland in 2015."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is a banana plant not a tree?", ["It has no wood; its trunk is wrapped leaf bases","It grows only in tropical soil, never in cooler places","It produces no flowers, only fruit","Its fruit grows underground like a root"], 0, "The pseudostem is made of tightly wrapped leaf bases, not wood."],
    [1, "Why is every plant in a banana plantation genetically alike?", ["Wild bananas have no variation","Pollen from one plant fertilises all the rest","Sterile plants are grown from cuttings","Farmers breed them in laboratories from seed"], 2, "Cultivated bananas cannot breed normally, so each new plant is a clone of its parent."],
    [2, "Why did Panama disease devastate the Gros Michel?", ["It was grown only in cold climates","Every plant was a clone, so none had resistance","The fungus spread through shipping boxes","Its skin was too thin to protect it"], 1, "A uniform crop has no genetic variety, so one disease can sweep through all of it."],
    [3, "What triggers a banana to ripen?", ["Cold air in the display fridge","Ethylene, a gas the fruit makes itself","Direct sunlight falling on the skin","Moisture building up inside the peel"], 1, "Ethylene starts the conversion of starch to sugar, and extra ethylene speeds the process."],
    [4, "Why is Tropical Race 4 a worry for Queensland growers?", ["It attacks the Cavendish, the main variety grown","It makes the fruit unsafe to eat after harvest","It spreads only through cyclone winds and rain","It only affects bananas imported from overseas"], 0, "Tropical Race 4 is a Panama disease strain that threatens the Cavendish, so hygiene helps contain it."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The banana in your lunchbox is a copy. Why that makes it so fragile."});

WB.L("potato-andes-world", "food", "beginner", "The potato: from the Andes to everywhere",
  "A knobbly tuber from the high Andes fed empires, sparked suspicion in Europe, and became one of the world's most important foods.",
  [
    ["Born in the Andes", "The potato was domesticated in the high Andes, and Andean peoples developed thousands of varieties and a way to store it for years.",
      "The potato we eat was domesticated in the Andes, near Lake Titicaca in southern Peru and northern Bolivia, perhaps 7,000 to 10,000 years ago. Farmers grew food above 3,000 metres, with frost, thin air and short seasons. They bred thousands of varieties, from purple and red to gold and near black, each suited to particular soils, heights and uses. The International Potato Center in Lima keeps a huge collection as insurance for the future.\n\nFresh potatoes rot, so Andean peoples invented chuño. Potatoes freeze overnight on the ground, thaw in the sun and are trodden to squeeze out water. The light, hard result lasts for years, and the Inca state stored it in roadside warehouses."],
    ["Arriving in Europe", "Europeans distrusted the potato at first, but its high yields eventually won farmers over, helped by Parmentier's promotion in France.",
      "Spanish soldiers met the potato in the 1530s, and by the 1560s and 1570s it had crossed the Atlantic. Europeans were wary. It belongs to the nightshade family, and its leaves and green parts really do contain toxic glycoalkaloids, including solanine. Gradually farmers noticed that potatoes gave far more calories per hectare than wheat or rye, grew in poor soil and cool climates, and could stay in the ground until needed.\n\nIn France, the army pharmacist Antoine-Augustin Parmentier had survived on potatoes as a prisoner of war. He promoted them tirelessly, and in 1772 the Paris Faculty of Medicine declared them fit to eat. A story says he guarded a potato field by day but not by night, so locals would steal it. That tale may be embellished."],
    ["Ireland's Great Famine", "Relying on one potato variety left Ireland exposed when blight struck in 1845.",
      "By the 1840s, much of Ireland's rural poor relied on potatoes for most of their food. Many grew a single high-yielding variety, the Lumper, so the crop was genetically very uniform. In 1845 late blight, caused by the fungus-like Phytophthora infestans, turned potatoes black and rotten in the ground, and it returned in following years.\n\nRoughly a million people died of starvation and disease, and more than a million emigrated, many to North America, Britain and Australia. Political choices made it worse: food continued to be exported from Ireland, and relief was inadequate. The famine is a lasting lesson about the danger of depending on a single crop."],
    ["A global staple", "Potatoes feed the world efficiently and are more nutritious than their reputation, if not deep fried and loaded with fat and salt.",
      "The potato spread across Europe, Asia and Africa, and is now one of the most important food crops on Earth. As of the mid-2020s, China grows the most, followed by India. Potatoes give more food per hectare than most grains and need less water, which matters as populations grow and the climate changes.\n\nThey are also more nutritious than their reputation. A potato provides energy as starch, along with fibre, potassium and a useful amount of vitamin C, with much of the fibre in the skin. It is usually what we do to potatoes, such as deep frying and adding salt, butter and cream, that makes them less healthy, not the potato itself."],
    ["Cooking and storing them", "Starchy potatoes suit mash and chips, waxy ones suit salads, and green or bitter potatoes should be trimmed or thrown out.",
      "Potatoes fall into two camps. Floury or starchy varieties, such as russets, break apart when cooked and suit fluffy mash, roasts and chips. Waxy varieties, such as kipflers and small new potatoes, hold their shape for salads and boiling. The difference comes from starch content and how the cells hold together.\n\nStore potatoes somewhere cool, dark and dry. Light turns them green as they make chlorophyll, and alongside it they can make more glycoalkaloids, which taste bitter and can cause illness in large amounts. Cut away green patches and sprouts generously, and throw out potatoes that are very green or taste bitter. Keeping them out of the light is the simplest fix."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does chuño preserve potatoes?", ["They are smoked over open fires for several weeks","They are buried in damp soil until they ferment","They are salted and then dried in the shade","They are frozen overnight, thawed in sun and trodden dry"], 3, "Repeated freezing, thawing and treading removes water, giving a potato that lasts for years."],
    [1, "Why did farmers eventually adopt the potato despite their suspicion?", ["It produced far more calories per hectare, even in poor soil","It was cheaper to import than wheat from the colonies","It had no toxic parts, so cooks trusted it","It tasted better than bread to most Europeans"], 0, "High yields in poor soils and cool climates outweighed Europeans' early wariness."],
    [2, "What made Ireland so vulnerable to potato blight in 1845?", ["Heavy reliance on one genetically uniform potato variety","Poor soil that could grow nothing else","A shortage of farmers after heavy emigration","A ban on growing other vegetables"], 0, "A uniform crop has no variety to resist disease, so dependence on it was dangerous."],
    [3, "What usually makes potato dishes less healthy?", ["The starch that is found inside the potato","How they are prepared, such as deep frying","Their lack of fibre and other nutrients","The vitamin C that sits in their skin"], 1, "Potatoes themselves offer starch, fibre, potassium and vitamin C. Preparation is the issue."],
    [4, "What should you do with a potato that has green patches?", ["Peel off only the brown spots and cook as normal","Boil it for longer to neutralise the toxins","Cut away the green generously, or bin very green ones","Freeze it until the green fades to white"], 2, "Green patches can signal more glycoalkaloids, which can cause illness in large amounts."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Europe feared it, then one crop's failure killed a million. The potato's story."});

WB.L("rice-staple-grain", "food", "beginner", "Rice: the grain that feeds billions",
  "Billions of people eat rice every day. Its story runs from ancient Chinese wetlands to a ‘miracle rice’ and paddies in the New South Wales Riverina.",
  [
    ["Two origins, flooded fields", "Asian and African rice were domesticated separately, and flooding paddies helps rice outcompete weeds.",
      "Most rice is Asian rice, Oryza sativa, domesticated in the Yangtze region of China roughly 9,000 years ago, though researchers still debate how japonica and indica arose. Farmers selected plants whose grains stayed on the stalk, easing harvest. Separately, West Africans domesticated African rice, Oryza glaberrima, in the Niger River delta around 3,000 years ago. It is hardy and still grown, though Asian rice has largely replaced it.\n\nRice does not need to be underwater, but it tolerates flooding because air channels carry oxygen to its roots. Weeds struggle in flooded soil, so farmers flood paddies to control them. The downside is methane from microbes in flooded soil, which farmers try to cut by alternate wetting and drying."],
    ["Sticky or fluffy", "The texture of cooked rice depends mainly on two starches, amylose and amylopectin.",
      "Rice starch comes in two forms. Amylose is a straight chain, and rices high in it, such as many long-grain varieties, cook up firm with separate grains. Amylopectin is highly branched and makes rice soft and sticky. Japanese short-grain rice has relatively little amylose, so it clumps nicely for sushi. Risotto rices such as arborio release creamy starch when stirred. Sticky or glutinous rice, used in Thai and Lao cooking, is almost entirely amylopectin and contains no gluten; glutinous means glue-like. Jasmine tends to be softer and stickier than basmati. Rinsing removes surface starch and gives looser grains.\n\nThere are two classic methods: absorption, where grains soak up a measured amount of water, and boiling in plenty of water that is drained. Electric rice cookers have been popular in Japan since the mid-1950s."],
    ["White rice and vitamins", "Polishing rice removes the vitamins in its bran, and studying the resulting disease helped reveal that vitamins exist.",
      "Brown rice keeps its bran and germ, which hold fibre, oils, minerals and B vitamins. White rice has them polished away, so it cooks faster and lasts longer but is less nutritious. As steam-powered mills spread polished rice across Asia in the late nineteenth century, beriberi became widespread, causing nerve damage, weakness and heart failure.\n\nIn the Dutch East Indies, now Indonesia, the physician Christiaan Eijkman noticed in the 1890s that chickens fed polished rice developed similar symptoms and recovered on unpolished rice. His work helped show that tiny amounts of certain substances in food are essential. The missing substance proved to be thiamine, vitamin B1, and Eijkman shared the 1929 Nobel Prize."],
    ["Miracle rice, thirsty crops", "Short, sturdy IR8 rice boosted yields across Asia, but modern rice farming also strains water and the environment.",
      "In the 1960s, experts feared mass famine in Asia as populations outgrew harvests. At the International Rice Research Institute in the Philippines, breeders crossed a tall Indonesian variety with a Taiwanese dwarf. The result, IR8, released in 1966, had short, stiff stems. Tall varieties fell over under heavy fertiliser, but IR8 carried heavy heads of grain and often yielded far more. Known as \"miracle rice\", it was central to the Green Revolution. The costs included heavier use of fertiliser, pesticides and water, and the loss of many traditional varieties.\n\nAustralia has grown rice commercially for about a century, mainly in the Riverina, on Murrumbidgee and Murray water. Growers achieve some of the world's highest yields, but the crop swings with drought, collapsing in very dry years."],
    ["Leftover rice safety", "Cooked rice must be cooled quickly and refrigerated because heat-resistant Bacillus cereus spores can make toxins.",
      "Rice can carry spores of Bacillus cereus, which survive cooking. If cooked rice sits warm for hours, the spores grow and make toxins that reheating will not destroy, causing vomiting.\n\nSo cool leftover rice quickly, refrigerate it within an hour or so, eat it within a day or so and reheat it until steaming hot. Spreading rice on a tray helps it cool faster. The risk comes from the time the rice spends warm, not from the rice itself, which is a safe, healthy staple for billions of people when handled sensibly."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do rice paddies work well as flooded fields?", ["Rice cannot grow without being fully underwater","Water makes rice grains heavier and easier to harvest","Weeds struggle in flooded soil, but rice tolerates it","Flooding adds nutrients that other crops need"], 2, "Air channels let rice tolerate flooding, which helps it outcompete weeds."],
    [1, "What makes sticky rice sticky?", ["It contains a lot of gluten","It is almost entirely amylopectin","It is never rinsed or soaked","It is very high in amylose"], 1, "Highly branched amylopectin makes rice soft and clumpy, and sticky rice has no gluten."],
    [2, "What caused beriberi to spread with polished rice?", ["Polishing removed the bran, which held thiamine","Steam mills contaminated the rice with bacteria","White rice was too high in starch","Polishing added toxins to the grain"], 0, "The bran holds vitamin B1, so polished rice left people short of thiamine."],
    [3, "Why did IR8 rice raise yields so much?", ["Short, stiff stems let it carry heavy grain upright","It was grown in flooded fields all year round","It needed far less water than older varieties","It had tall stalks that caught more sunlight"], 0, "Short stems let IR8 take heavy fertiliser and carry more grain."],
    [4, "Why should leftover rice be cooled and refrigerated quickly?", ["Cold rice quickly loses most of its vitamins","Reheating cooked rice makes the grains hard","Spores survive cooking and make toxins in warm rice","Rice absorbs smells from other food in the fridge"], 2, "The toxins from Bacillus cereus survive reheating, so limiting time warm is the key."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"One grain feeds billions, and one pan of leftovers can make you ill. Why?"});

WB.L("cookware-pans-guide", "food", "beginner", "Pots and pans: what your cookware is doing",
  "Cast iron, stainless steel, non-stick and copper each handle heat differently. Know their personalities and your cooking gets easier overnight.",
  [
    ["Spreading and holding heat", "Good cookware spreads heat evenly and holds enough of it that cold food does not crash the pan's temperature.",
      "A pan has two main heat jobs. The first is conducting heat sideways from the burner, so the whole base is evenly hot instead of scorching in the middle with cool edges. Metals vary hugely: copper conducts roughly twenty-five times better than stainless steel, and aluminium is not far behind copper.\n\nThe second job is storing heat. A thick, heavy pan holds more energy, so when you drop in a cold steak the temperature does not crash and you still get a good sear. Cast iron is a poor conductor but a great heat store. No single material does everything perfectly, which is why cooks collect a few different pans for different tasks."],
    ["Cast iron and carbon steel", "Cast iron and carbon steel heat slowly, hold heat well, and gain a non-stick seasoning of baked-on oil.",
      "Cast iron is heavy, nearly indestructible and can last for generations. It heats slowly and unevenly, so preheat it gently for several minutes. Once hot it holds heat beautifully, ideal for searing and frying. Its seasoning is not grime. When thin layers of oil are heated past their smoke point, the oil polymerises into a hard, slick coating bonded to the metal.\n\nA popular myth says never to use detergent. Modern dishwashing liquid is mild and will not strip well-polymerised seasoning, although soaking or a dishwasher can cause rust. Dry the pan straight away and wipe on a little oil.\n\nCarbon steel is close to cast iron in chemistry but rolled thinner, so it heats faster and is much lighter. Woks and omelette pans are often carbon steel, and are seasoned too."],
    ["Stainless, copper and aluminium", "Stainless steel is tough and non-reactive but needs an aluminium or copper core to heat evenly.",
      "Stainless steel resists rust, does not react with acidic foods like tomato or wine, and can go from stovetop to oven. On its own it conducts heat poorly, so good pans are clad, with aluminium or copper sandwiched between steel. Food sticks when the pan is not hot enough, because proteins bond to the metal. Flick in a drop of water: if it skitters as a bead, the pan is hot, thanks to the Leidenfrost effect, where a cushion of steam holds the droplet up. Then add oil and food.\n\nCopper is the most responsive metal, heating and cooling almost instantly, and is usually lined with tin or stainless steel because it reacts with some foods. Plain aluminium is light, cheap and conductive but reacts with acidic foods and dents easily. Hard-anodised aluminium is tougher."],
    ["Non-stick, and using it safely", "Non-stick coatings are stable at normal cooking temperatures, but an empty pan overheated on high can release harmful fumes.",
      "In 1938 the DuPont chemist Roy Plunkett was experimenting with refrigerant gases. One cylinder seemed empty, but cut open it held a waxy white solid: the gas had polymerised into PTFE, a material so slippery and unreactive that almost nothing sticks to it. In the 1950s the French engineer Marc Grégoire bonded PTFE to aluminium, reportedly at his wife Colette's suggestion, creating Tefal.\n\nAt ordinary temperatures PTFE is inert. Above roughly 260 degrees Celsius it breaks down, releasing fumes that can cause flu-like symptoms in people and can kill pet birds. An empty pan on high can get that hot within minutes, so cook on low to medium heat and replace badly scratched pans. PFOA, a chemical once used to make PTFE, raised health and environmental concerns and has been phased out."],
    ["Induction and what to buy", "Induction cooktops need magnetic pans, and a few well-chosen pieces beat a large matching set.",
      "An induction cooktop does not heat a coil or flame. A rapidly changing magnetic field makes electric currents flow inside the pan's base, so the pan itself becomes the heat source. That works only with magnetic metals such as cast iron, carbon steel and many stainless steels. Plain aluminium, copper and glass will not work without a magnetic base. A quick test is whether a fridge magnet sticks firmly to the bottom.\n\nWhen building a kitchen, a few good pieces usually beat a big boxed set. A heavy frying pan in cast iron or carbon steel, a non-stick pan for eggs, a clad stainless saucepan and a big pot for stock and pasta cover most cooking."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does a heavy pan help when you add a cold steak?", ["It conducts heat sideways faster than copper","It holds more heat, so it stays hot","It stops the steak from sticking","It reflects heat back into the food"], 1, "A thick pan holds more energy, so cold food does not cool it much and you still get a good sear."],
    [1, "What is cast iron seasoning?", ["A mineral salt rubbed into the surface","Burnt food residue that builds up over time","A layer of oil baked into a hard coating","A factory-applied plastic film"], 2, "Oil heated past its smoke point polymerises into a hard, slick coating, not grime."],
    [2, "Why are good stainless steel pans clad with aluminium or copper?", ["Stainless steel conducts heat poorly on its own","Stainless steel rusts quickly","Stainless steel is too light to hold heat","Stainless steel reacts with tomato"], 0, "The aluminium or copper core spreads heat evenly through a pan that is otherwise a poor conductor."],
    [3, "What is the main safety risk with non-stick pans?", ["Small flakes of coating are toxic if swallowed","The coating dissolves in acidic foods","Cooking on medium heat damages the coating","An empty pan on high heat can release fumes"], 3, "PTFE is stable at normal temperatures but breaks down above roughly 260 degrees Celsius."],
    [4, "How can you check if a pan will work on an induction cooktop?", ["See whether it is heavier than a kilogram","Check that it has a non-stick coating","Look for a copper base","Check that a magnet sticks to its base"], 3, "Induction needs a magnetic base, and a magnet sticking firmly shows it has one."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Left empty on high, a non-stick pan can turn hazardous in minutes. Why?"});

WB.L("sugar-bitter-history", "food", "intermediate", "Sugar: a sweet substance with a bitter past",
  "Sugar began as a rare spice and a medicine. Its rise to everyday sweetness was driven by slavery, wars and, in Queensland, a painful chapter of Australian history.",
  [
    ["From reed to luxury", "Sugarcane, first domesticated in New Guinea, became a rare spice and medicine before Europe's supplies grew and it turned into an everyday staple.",
      "Sugarcane is a giant grass first domesticated in New Guinea thousands of years ago. People chewed the stalks for their sweet juice. In India, they learned to boil the juice down into crystals, at least by the early centuries AD. The word sugar descends from the Sanskrit sharkara, and candy from khanda. Greek writers who met Alexander the Great's forces in India described a reed that made honey without bees.\n\nArab and Persian cultivators spread cane and refining across the Mediterranean, and to medieval Europeans sugar was a luxury used in medicine and feasts. That changed when Europeans grew cane on Atlantic islands such as Madeira, then in the Americas: Columbus took it to Hispaniola in 1493. Prices fell and demand soared, and in Britain sweetened tea became part of daily life."],
    ["Slavery and the boycott", "Sugar plantations ran on the enslavement of millions of Africans, and British consumers eventually boycotted slave-grown sugar.",
      "Sugar was hard, dangerous work. Cane had to be crushed and boiled quickly after cutting, so mills and boiling houses ran around the clock. Brazil and then Caribbean islands such as Barbados and Jamaica became huge producers, and owners met their labour demand through the transatlantic slave trade. Millions of enslaved Africans were shipped to the Americas, many to sugar colonies where death rates were terrible. Historians see sugar as a central engine of the slave economy.\n\nIn the early 1790s, British pamphlets urged families to stop buying slave-grown sugar, and around 300,000 people took part. Women, who largely controlled household shopping but could not vote, were central. Britain abolished its slave trade in 1807 and slavery in most colonies from 1834. The boycott is an early example of ethical consumption."],
    ["Napoleon and the beet", "Sugar from beets is chemically identical to cane sugar, and war-time blockades turned beet sugar into a major industry.",
      "In 1747 the German chemist Andreas Marggraf showed that sugar crystals from beets were identical to cane sugar. His student Franz Achard bred beets with more sugar and opened the first beet sugar factory in Silesia in 1801.\n\nWar made the difference. During the Napoleonic Wars, the British naval blockade cut France off from Caribbean cane sugar. Napoleon backed beet sugar with land grants, funding and decrees in the early 1810s, and factories sprang up. The industry survived the war, and a significant share of the world's sugar now comes from beets grown in cooler climates. Chemically, white sugar from cane and from beet is the same molecule: sucrose."],
    ["Queensland and blackbirding", "Queensland's sugar industry relied on tens of thousands of Pacific Islander labourers, many tricked or forced into coming.",
      "Sugar became a major Queensland industry from the 1860s. Planters wanted cheap labour for hot, punishing work, and between 1863 and 1904 around 60,000 people from Pacific islands, especially today's Vanuatu and Solomon Islands, were brought to Queensland under labour contracts. Some came willingly, but many were tricked, coerced or kidnapped, a practice known as blackbirding. Wages were low, conditions harsh and death rates high.\n\nAfter Federation, the Pacific Island Labourers Act of 1901 led to the deportation of most Islanders, as part of the White Australia policy. Some stayed, and their descendants form the Australian South Sea Islander community, formally recognised by the Commonwealth in 1994. The cane toad was also introduced in 1935 to fight cane beetles."],
    ["Sugar and your body", "Sugar is not poison, but the World Health Organization advises keeping free sugars below 10 percent of daily energy.",
      "Table sugar, sucrose, is one glucose and one fructose unit joined together, and your gut quickly splits it apart. Honey, maple syrup, coconut sugar and raw sugar are mostly the same simple sugars in slightly different proportions, with only trace amounts of anything else, so they are not much healthier.\n\nThe World Health Organization recommends keeping free sugars below 10 percent of daily energy, and suggests below 5 percent brings further benefits. Free sugars are those added to food, plus those in honey, syrups and juices. Sugars naturally inside whole fruit, milk and vegetables are not counted. Sugary drinks are the biggest single source for many people, and they are strongly linked to tooth decay and weight gain."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did sugar change from a rare luxury to an everyday staple in Europe?", ["More cane grown in the Americas lowered prices","Cheaper Asian imports arrived by overland routes","Sugar stopped being used in medicine and feasts","Arab traders began selling it more cheaply"], 0, "As cane growing expanded in the Atlantic and Americas, prices fell and demand soared."],
    [1, "What did the British sugar boycott of the 1790s show?", ["Women could organise only through voting","Sugar could not be produced without slavery","Households could act by changing what they bought","Slavery ended in the colonies immediately"], 2, "Households, especially women, used shopping choices to oppose slavery, an early form of ethical consumption."],
    [2, "Why did the Napoleonic Wars boost beet sugar?", ["Beet sugar was chemically better than cane","The British blockade cut France off from cane","Cane sugar was banned across Europe","Beets were easier to carry on ships"], 1, "Cut off from cane, France backed beet sugar, and the industry outlasted the war."],
    [3, "What was blackbirding?", ["A law banning the burning of cane fields","A trade in tropical bird feathers","Tricking or forcing Pacific Islanders into labour","A tax on sugar sold in Queensland"], 2, "Many Islanders brought to Queensland's cane fields were tricked, coerced or kidnapped."],
    [4, "Why are honey and raw sugar not much healthier than table sugar?", ["They contain more sucrose than cane sugar","They are mostly the same simple sugars","The World Health Organization does not count them","They are digested more slowly"], 1, "These sweeteners are mostly the same sugars, and honey is counted as free sugar."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Sugar once sat with spices in a pharmacy. How did it become so common?"});

WB.L("escoffier-modern-kitchen", "food", "intermediate", "Escoffier: the chef who organised the kitchen",
  "Every time a cook answers ‘Yes, chef’, they echo one man. Auguste Escoffier turned chaotic kitchens into disciplined teams, and named a dessert after an Australian soprano.",
  [
    ["Order from chaos", "Before Escoffier, grand kitchens were hot, disorganised places where cooks duplicated work.",
      "Georges Auguste Escoffier was born in 1846 in Villeneuve-Loubet, near Nice. At around thirteen he began an apprenticeship in his uncle's restaurant, then worked his way up through Paris kitchens. During the Franco-Prussian War of 1870 he cooked for the French army, an experience of feeding large numbers under pressure that many writers think shaped his ideas about organisation.\n\nKitchens of the time were brutal. Coal-fired ranges made them extremely hot, ventilation was poor and cooks often drank heavily through long shifts. One cook might spend hours making a whole dish while another did nearly the same beside him. Service à la russe, with courses brought out one after another, was replacing service à la française, with many dishes placed on the table together, and it demanded tighter timing."],
    ["The brigade system", "Escoffier organised kitchens into specialised stations in a clear chain of command, so complex dishes could be assembled in minutes.",
      "Escoffier's lasting innovation was the brigade de cuisine, a kitchen run like a well-drilled team. At the top sits the chef de cuisine, with a sous chef second in command. Each station is led by a chef de partie: the saucier handles sauces and sautéed dishes, the rôtisseur roasts, the poissonnier cooks fish, the entremetier handles vegetables and soups, the garde manger prepares cold foods and the pâtissier makes pastry and desserts. Junior cooks, called commis, work under each leader.\n\nBecause stations prepare components in parallel, a complex dish can be assembled in minutes rather than hours. He also pushed for calmer behaviour, discouraging shouting, drinking and smoking on duty, and insisting on clean uniforms. The structure and its French titles are still used worldwide, including in Australia."],
    ["Ritz and the Savoy", "Escoffier's partnership with hotelier César Ritz made dining out in elegant hotels fashionable, including for women.",
      "In Monte Carlo, Escoffier met the Swiss hotelier César Ritz, and the two formed one of history's great hospitality partnerships. In 1890 they took charge of London's new Savoy Hotel, where Escoffier's cooking drew royalty, aristocrats and celebrities. They helped make dining in an elegant hotel restaurant fashionable, including for well-to-do women, who had been less likely to dine in public.\n\nThe Savoy partnership ended in 1898, when Ritz and Escoffier were dismissed amid a dispute with the owners over alleged financial irregularities. They moved on quickly. Ritz opened his famous Paris hotel that year, with Escoffier running the kitchen, and the pair then opened the Carlton Hotel in London, where Escoffier cooked for many years."],
    ["Peach Melba", "Escoffier created Peach Melba to honour Australian soprano Nellie Melba, one of the great opera stars of her day.",
      "Escoffier liked to name dishes after famous guests, and his best-known tribute was to an Australian. Nellie Melba, born Helen Porter Mitchell in Melbourne, took her stage name from her home city and became one of the most celebrated sopranos of her time. While she performed in London in the 1890s, Escoffier created a dessert in her honour: poached peaches served on vanilla ice cream.\n\nAn early presentation was reportedly served in a swan carved from ice, a nod to the opera Lohengrin. Raspberry sauce joined the classic recipe a few years later. Melba toast, crisp thin slices of dry toast, is also traditionally linked to the singer and Escoffier's kitchens."],
    ["The guide and the legacy", "Escoffier's Le Guide Culinaire simplified French cooking into a system, and his kitchen model still runs restaurants today.",
      "In 1903 Escoffier published Le Guide Culinaire, written with colleagues, which gathered thousands of recipes into a single reference still read by chefs. Marie-Antoine Carême had earlier grouped French sauces. Escoffier's system is usually summarised as five mother sauces: béchamel, velouté, espagnole, hollandaise and tomato. Learn these and you can make hundreds of variations. He also championed simplicity over towering decoration, his motto often given as \"faites simple\", focused on flavour rather than display.\n\nHe supported charities for retired cooks and worked to make cooking respected as a skilled career. He was later made a knight of the Legion of Honour and died in 1935, aged 88. His influence lives on in stations, titles and the call-and-response of \"Yes, chef\", and in the idea that a kitchen should run calmly, cleanly and on time."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What problem did Escoffier face in nineteenth century kitchens?", ["Too few cooks to prepare meals","Cooks unfamiliar with French recipes","Banned use of coal-fired stoves","Hot, chaotic kitchens with duplicated work"], 3, "Work was loosely organised, so cooks overlapped and service suffered."],
    [1, "Why does the brigade system speed up service?", ["Stations prepare parts of a dish in parallel","One cook makes each dish from start to finish","Dishes are cooked ahead of time and then reheated","Customers choose from a much shorter menu"], 0, "Specialised stations working at once turn hours of work into minutes."],
    [2, "What did Escoffier and Ritz help change about dining out?", ["They moved fine dining out of hotels","They made hotel dining fashionable, women included","They replaced restaurants with banquets","They made meals cheaper for ordinary people"], 1, "Their Savoy partnership made hotel restaurants a stylish place for society to dine."],
    [3, "What is Peach Melba made of?", ["Raspberry sauce over toast","Poached peaches on vanilla ice cream","Peaches baked in pastry","Sugar-coated peaches in cream"], 1, "Escoffier created the dessert of peaches on vanilla ice cream for the soprano Nellie Melba."],
    [4, "What is the idea behind the mother sauces?", ["A few base sauces can be varied into hundreds","Every dish needs its own separate sauce","Sauces should be left out of simple cooking","Each chef must invent their own signature sauce"], 0, "Five base sauces can be varied with added ingredients to produce many others."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Why every kitchen shouts the same reply, and who put it there."});

WB.L("kitchen-myths-busted", "food", "intermediate", "Kitchen myths, busted",
  "Searing seals in juices. Alcohol burns off. Oil stops pasta sticking. These ideas get passed from cook to cook, and many of them are simply wrong.",
  [
    ["Searing seals nothing", "Searing adds flavour but does not seal in juices; keeping meat juicy comes from not overcooking it.",
      "The idea that searing ‘seals in the juices’ goes back to the German chemist Justus von Liebig in the mid-1800s and spread through cookbooks for a century. Experiments have repeatedly shown it is wrong. A seared crust is not waterproof, and seared meat loses about as much moisture as unseared meat cooked to the same internal temperature, sometimes more. The sizzle in the pan is moisture boiling away.\n\nSearing is still worth doing, because the Maillard reaction creates deep browned flavour and appealing colour. What keeps meat juicy is not overcooking it, which is why a thermometer matters far more than a dramatic sear."],
    ["Alcohol lingers in food", "A surprising share of alcohol can remain in cooked food, depending on time and method.",
      "Many people believe all the alcohol in wine or spirits disappears as soon as a dish is heated. United States Department of Agriculture measurements show otherwise. When alcohol was added to a boiling liquid and removed from the heat, most of it remained. Flaming a dish left around three-quarters. After 15 minutes of simmering, about 40 percent was still there, and even after two and a half hours, around 5 percent remained.\n\nAlcohol and water evaporate together, so it never vanishes all at once. The amounts per serve are usually small, but it matters for people who avoid alcohol completely for health, religious or recovery reasons, and for young children."],
    ["Pasta water myths", "Oil in pasta water does little, and salt gives flavour rather than faster boiling.",
      "Pouring oil into pasta water ‘to stop it sticking’ does almost nothing. Oil and water do not mix, so the oil sits on the surface while the pasta tumbles below. Pasta sticks in the first couple of minutes, when surface starch turns gluey. Use plenty of water at a rolling boil and stir well early on. Oil that coats drained pasta can make sauce slide off. Save a cup of the starchy water instead, which helps sauces turn glossy.\n\nSalt does not make water boil faster either. Dissolved salt slightly raises the boiling point, but at cooking amounts the change is a fraction of a degree. The burst of bubbles when salt hits hot water is crystals giving gases a place to form. The real reason to salt is flavour: food absorbs salt as it cooks, seasoning it from within."],
    ["Microwaves and nutrients", "Microwaves heat only the outer centimetre or two of food, and quick microwaving often keeps vitamins well.",
      "A popular belief says microwaves cook from the inside out. In fact they penetrate only a centimetre or two into most foods, making water molecules jiggle and generate heat. The centre of a large item warms as heat spreads inwards, just as in an oven, only from a thicker starting layer. That is why recipes include standing time to let heat even out, and why stirring and rotating help avoid cold spots, which matter for food safety when reheating.\n\nAnother myth is that microwaving destroys nutrients. Because cooking is quick and uses little water, microwaving vegetables often keeps water-soluble vitamins, such as vitamin C, as well as or better than boiling."],
    ["Dropped food and frozen food", "Bacteria can transfer to dropped food in under a second, and frozen produce can be as nutritious as fresh.",
      "The five-second rule claims food is safe if picked up quickly. In 2016, food scientist Donald Schaffner's team at Rutgers University contaminated surfaces including stainless steel, tile, wood and carpet with bacteria, then dropped watermelon, bread, buttered bread and gummy lollies. Transfer could happen in under a second. Longer contact meant more bacteria, but speed alone did not make food safe. Moisture mattered most: watermelon picked up the most, dry lollies the least. Carpet transferred fewer bacteria than tile or steel.\n\nFresh is not always better than frozen either. Fresh vegetables can spend days in transport and your fridge, losing vitamins. Frozen vegetables are blanched and frozen soon after harvest, and studies generally find similar nutrients. Frozen peas, berries and spinach are cheap and waste less, while fresh often wins on texture and taste."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What actually keeps a steak juicy?", ["Searing it first to seal the surface","Resting it in oil before cooking","Not overcooking it","Cooking it at the highest heat possible"], 2, "Searing does not seal in juices, so avoiding overcooking is what matters."],
    [1, "What happens to alcohol when you simmer it in a dish?", ["Some remains, even after long cooking","It all evaporates within a few minutes","It turns into sugar as it heats","It stays at the same level however long you cook"], 0, "Even after two and a half hours of simmering, around 5 percent of alcohol remained."],
    [2, "What is the real reason to salt pasta water?", ["It seasons the pasta as it cooks","It makes the water boil faster","It stops the pasta sticking","It stops the water boiling over"], 0, "Salt raises the boiling point only slightly, so its job is seasoning from within."],
    [3, "Why do microwave recipes include standing time?", ["The food keeps cooking from the inside out","Heat spreads inwards from the outer layer and evens out","The microwaves keep working after the oven stops","The food needs to cool before it is safe to eat"], 1, "Microwaves heat only the outer layer, and the centre warms as that heat spreads inwards."],
    [4, "What did the Rutgers study find about the five-second rule?", ["Dry foods are always safe if picked up quickly","Bacteria transfer only after several seconds","Carpet always transferred the most bacteria","Transfer takes under a second, and wet foods pick up most"], 3, "Speed does not make dropped food safe; moisture mattered most."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Searing doesn't seal in juices. What does keep meat moist?"});

WB.L("aussie-food-icons", "food", "intermediate", "Pavlova, lamingtons and pies: Australia's food icons",
  "Who really invented the pavlova? Why can't you sell an ‘Anzac cookie’? The stories behind Australia's favourite foods are full of rivalries, legends and law.",
  [
    ["Contested origins", "Pavlova and lamingtons are Australian icons whose true origins are disputed or unknown.",
      "The pavlova is named after the Russian ballerina Anna Pavlova, who toured Australia and New Zealand in 1926 and 1929. Australians long credited Bert Sachse of Perth's Esplanade Hotel with creating it in 1935. But the New Zealand food historian Helen Leach found New Zealand recipes called pavlova from the late 1920s. She also found similar meringue cakes in Europe and the United States long before either country named one after a dancer. The friendly argument is unlikely ever to end.\n\nThe lamington is a sponge square dipped in chocolate icing and rolled in desiccated coconut. It is named after Lord Lamington, Governor of Queensland from 1896 to 1901. The popular story credits his French-born chef, Armand Galland, with inventing it to use up stale sponge, but historians cannot confirm it."],
    ["Anzac biscuits and the law", "The word Anzac is protected by Australian law, and Anzac biscuits may only be sold under strict conditions.",
      "Anzac biscuits are made from rolled oats, flour, sugar, butter, golden syrup and usually desiccated coconut, with no eggs. They are linked to the First World War, but historians think oat biscuits were mainly baked and sold at home to raise money for the war effort, not standard soldiers' food. Troops were issued a notoriously hard ration biscuit, jokingly called an Anzac tile or wafer.\n\nThe name Anzac itself is protected under Australian law, and using it commercially needs approval from the Minister for Veterans' Affairs. Products may be sold as Anzac biscuits if they stick to the traditional recipe and shape. Calling them ‘Anzac cookies’ is not normally allowed, because of the American association."],
    ["The meat pie", "The hand-held meat pie arrived from Britain and became an Australian favourite, with regional variations such as the pie floater.",
      "Australians are devoted to the meat pie: minced or chunky beef in gravy inside a pastry case, eaten by hand at football games, bakeries and service stations. The tradition came from Britain, but Australians made it their own, with brands, bakery competitions and fierce local loyalties.\n\nVariations abound, including curry pies, steak and kidney, and the pie with tomato sauce on top. Adelaide is famous for the pie floater, a meat pie served upside down in a bowl of thick green pea soup, often topped with tomato sauce and once sold from late-night pie carts. Debates about the right pastry, gravy and filling still run hot, and many bakeries now offer gourmet and vegetarian versions."],
    ["Borrowed and reinvented", "The Chiko Roll, dim sim and Tim Tam show Australian food borrowing from other cuisines and making something new.",
      "In 1951 the Bendigo boilermaker Frank McEncroe launched the Chiko Roll at the Wagga Wagga Agricultural Show. Inspired by Chinese spring rolls, it wrapped cabbage, celery, barley, carrot, beef and seasonings in thick, deep-fried pastry, tough enough to eat on the move. The Australian dim sim, loosely based on Cantonese dumplings such as siu mai, is usually credited to Melbourne's Wing Lee restaurant in the 1940s. It is larger and meatier, steamed or deep fried, and often eaten with soy sauce.\n\nIn the 1950s, Arnott's food technologist Ian Norris came across Britain's Penguin biscuit overseas. In 1964 Arnott's launched the Tim Tam, named after the racehorse that won the 1958 Kentucky Derby. Fans invented the ‘Tim Tam slam’, using the biscuit as a straw for hot drinks."],
    ["Becoming an icon", "National foods usually have many borrowed roots and legendary origin stories; what makes them iconic is the pride people build around them.",
      "Look closely at almost any national dish and you find borrowing. Meat pies came from Britain, pavlova from a long European meringue tradition, the Chiko Roll and dim sim from Chinese cooking, and the flat white from a cafe culture shaped by Italian and other migrants. Long before any of this, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples ate a rich range of native foods, an older story still.\n\nOrigin tales tend to be colourful and hard to verify: a chef inventing a cake for unexpected guests, a dessert created in one inspired moment. Historians who dig into old cookbooks and newspapers often find gradual change rather than one inventor. What makes a food iconic is less its birth than the memories, rituals and pride people build around it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the evidence suggest about the pavlova's origin?", ["Earlier recipes exist in New Zealand and Europe","It was invented in Perth in 1935 and nowhere earlier","It was first made in Russia for the ballerina herself","It was created by a Queensland governor's chef"], 0, "Earlier New Zealand recipes and older meringue cakes mean the Perth claim is not the whole story."],
    [1, "Why can't you normally sell ‘Anzac cookies’ in Australia?", ["The name is protected, and ‘cookie’ is American","Cookies cannot legally contain oats","The traditional recipe is a closely guarded secret","Cookies must be approved by the army before sale"], 0, "Anzac biscuits may use the protected name only if they follow the traditional recipe and shape."],
    [2, "What is a pie floater?", ["A pie with a thick gravy topping","A meat pie served in thick green pea soup","A pie baked inside a loaf of bread","A pie cooked in boiling water"], 1, "Adelaide's pie floater is served upside down in a bowl of pea soup, often topped with tomato sauce."],
    [3, "What do the Chiko Roll and dim sim show about Australian food?", ["They were copied exactly from overseas","They borrowed ideas and made them local","They were created by the army","They were designed to be eaten cold"], 1, "Both took inspiration from Chinese food and became something distinctly Australian."],
    [4, "What usually makes a food an icon, according to the lesson?", ["A single inventor and a documented date","A secret recipe protected by strict law","The memories and pride people build around it","A famous chef serving it in fine restaurants"], 2, "Foods become iconic through the meaning people give them, not because of a single origin."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Two countries claim the pavlova. The evidence may surprise both."});

WB.L("sous-vide-precision", "food", "advanced", "Cooking by degrees: the science of precise temperature",
  "A few degrees can turn silky meat into shoe leather. Understanding exactly what heat does to proteins explains sous vide, perfect eggs and why chefs swear by thermometers.",
  [
    ["Cooking is protein chemistry", "Heat changes the shape of proteins, and each protein sets at its own temperature, often only a few degrees apart.",
      "Proteins are long chains folded into specific shapes. Heat makes them vibrate until the weak bonds holding their shape give way. They unfold (denaturation), then tangle with their neighbours (coagulation). That is what turns runny egg white solid and translucent prawn flesh firm and opaque.\n\nThe crucial detail is that different proteins set at different temperatures. In an egg, ovotransferrin starts setting near 62 degrees, the yolk thickens around 65, and ovalbumin needs close to 80. Hold an egg at 63 to 65 degrees for about an hour and you get a custard-like yolk and a barely set white, as in Japanese onsen tamago, cooked in hot springs. Boiling water at 100 degrees overshoots everything."],
    ["What happens inside meat", "Meat firms as its proteins set, dries out as they squeeze out juice, and turns tender only as collagen melts into gelatine.",
      "Meat is mostly water held inside muscle proteins and wrapped in connective tissue made largely of collagen. The muscle protein myosin begins to set at around 50 degrees, so the meat firms but stays juicy. That is roughly the rare range.\n\nThrough the 60s, other proteins, including actin at about 66 degrees or above, contract and squeeze out moisture, and the meat turns noticeably drier. Collagen behaves differently. It shrinks at around 60 degrees, but given enough time at higher temperatures it slowly breaks down into gelatine. That gives braised meat its silky, falling-apart texture. Hence the cook's dilemma: tender cuts want low temperatures, while tough, collagen-rich cuts need long cooking."],
    ["Why sous vide works", "Sous vide cooks sealed food in water held at the exact target temperature, so it cannot overcook the usual way.",
      "Sous vide is French for 'under vacuum'. Food is sealed in a bag and cooked in a water bath held at an exact temperature. Its culinary use is usually traced to 1974, when chef Georges Pralus used it for foie gras, which lost far less fat under gentle heat. Food scientist Bruno Goussault later trained chefs in it. Home circulators became affordable in the 2010s.\n\nWater carries heat much faster than air, which is why you can briefly put a hand in a 200 degree oven but not in boiling water. Set the bath to 56 degrees for medium-rare steak and the food can never get hotter. It cooks evenly from edge to centre, with no grey band. Cooks then add a quick, hot sear for browning."],
    ["Time as well as temperature", "Bacteria die according to both temperature and time, so a lower temperature held longer can be as effective as a brief higher one.",
      "Food safety guidance usually gives one number, such as cooking poultry until the centre reaches 75 degrees, which kills bacteria like Salmonella almost instantly. But bacterial death really depends on temperature and time together. At lower temperatures bacteria still die, just more slowly.\n\nFood scientists have published tables showing that holding food at a lower temperature for long enough achieves the same reduction as a brief moment at a higher one. That is how sous vide cooks can safely serve juicier chicken. These methods need accurate equipment, correct holding times and an understanding of the science. For everyday home cooking, follow the official temperature guidance and check with a probe thermometer."],
    ["The risks and the thermometer", "Low temperature cooking carries real food safety risks, and a probe thermometer is the simplest tool for cooking precisely.",
      "Precise low temperature cooking has risks that conventional cooking avoids. In a sealed, airless bag, bacteria that thrive without oxygen, such as Clostridium botulinum, can become a hazard if food is held too long at too low a temperature or cooled slowly afterwards. Cooking below about 55 degrees for long periods is particularly risky. Eat cooked food promptly, or chill it quickly in iced water and refrigerate it.\n\nPregnant people, young children, older adults and those with weakened immunity are generally advised to avoid lightly cooked meat, fish and eggs. For any cook, the best tool is a probe thermometer. Once you know the internal temperature, you are no longer guessing, and dry chicken and overcooked steak become far rarer."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does an egg held at about 64 degrees have a custardy yolk and a soft white?", ["Each protein sets at its own temperature","The shell blocks most of the heat","Low heat draws water out of the yolk","The white contains no protein to set"], 0, "Some white proteins and the yolk set around 62 to 65 degrees, while ovalbumin needs nearer 80."],
    [1, "Why do tough, collagen-rich cuts need long, slow cooking?", ["Myosin needs several hours of heat to set","Collagen turns to gelatine only after hours of heat","Long cooking adds moisture to the meat","Collagen is a fat that must melt out"], 1, "Collagen slowly turns to gelatine, which gives braises their silky, tender texture."],
    [2, "Why can't food overcook in a sous vide bath in the usual sense?", ["The vacuum bag stops heat reaching the edges","Food cooks from the inside out as the bath circulates","The bath is set to the target, so food cannot get hotter","Ice is added to the water as the food cooks"], 2, "The bath is held at the target temperature, so the whole piece reaches that doneness and no further."],
    [3, "How can a lower cooking temperature still make food safe?", ["Bacteria cannot survive in a vacuum bag","Low temperatures make bacteria harmless","Only a temperature of 100 degrees ever kills bacteria","A longer hold kills as many bacteria as brief high heat"], 3, "Bacterial death depends on time and temperature together, so lower temperatures need longer holding times."],
    [4, "What is the most practical way for a home cook to cook precisely and safely?", ["Check with a probe thermometer","Cook by timing alone at a low heat","Taste the food as it cooks","Judge doneness by colour"], 0, "A probe thermometer takes the guesswork out, avoiding both dry food and unsafe undercooking."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A few degrees turn a silky egg yolk into chalk. Find out why."});

